body:
The wait remains pending after an abort or timeout if the current condition has not settled. If that condition later returns `true`, the wait succeeds even though it was already aborted. A condition that never settles makes the timeout ineffective indefinitely.

Reproduced on Node.js v22.23.3 against main at `6b5f7d53400cdfacf71c0813a26350a80103a7b5` (package version 6.0.0).

```js
import pWaitFor from 'p-wait-for';
import {setTimeout as delay} from 'node:timers/promises';

const controller = new AbortController();
let finishCondition;

const waiting = pWaitFor(() => new Promise(resolve => {
    finishCondition = resolve;
}), {signal: controller.signal});

controller.abort(new Error('cancelled'));

console.log(await Promise.race([
    waiting.then(() => 'fulfilled', error => error.message),
    delay(30).then(() => 'still pending'),
]));
// Actual: 'still pending'. Expected: 'cancelled'.

finishCondition(true);
console.log(await waiting.then(() => 'fulfilled despite abort', error => error.message));
// Actual: 'fulfilled despite abort'. Expected: rejection with the abort reason.
```

The same issue affects `timeout`: replacing the signal option with `{timeout: 10}` and racing against a 50 ms delay still prints `'still pending'`. Resolving the condition to `true` then fulfills the wait rather than rejecting with `TimeoutError`.

The operation awaits `condition()` directly; the combined signal is observed only before the loop or during sleeps between checks. Cancellation needs to be able to settle the outer wait while the condition is pending. This does not require cancelling the condition's underlying work, but its eventual fulfillment/rejection still needs a handler.

The existing test named `abort while condition is pending` returns `false` after a delay, so it reaches another sleep and does not cover an indefinitely pending condition or a late `true` result. Related to the older closed #27, but this reproduction concerns the returned promise not settling at its deadline, rather than polling continuing after rejection.

I ran focused abort and timeout regressions locally; both fail on this checkout. I did not run the full project suite. No PR attached.
